  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1054 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\n00Z upper air analysis places a 599 dm high over the Lower\nMississippi Valley. This upper-level high has brought a\ncontinuation of the very hot temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions to the Mid-South into this weekend Late evening\ntemperatures still remain quite warm in the 80s at most locations\nwith surface dewpoints in the 70s.\n\nAnother very hot and oppressive day is expected across the Mid-\nSouth on Sunday as high pressure remains in place across the\nregion. Surface dewpoints are not anticipated to mix out as much\nas they did on Saturday with heat index values rising again to\n110 degrees or higher during peak heating. Thus, the Extreme Heat\nWarning will remain in effect through at least 8 PM Sunday\nevening. There is a very low chance (<10%) for isolated\nthunderstorms over portions of the Missouri Bootheel and\nnortheast Arkansas late Sunday afternoon into early Sunday\nevening. However, confidence in occurrence and coverage remains\nlow with convective temperatures at or above 100 degrees.\n\nLatest short-term model trends indicate the upper-level high will\nbegin to retrograde Sunday night into Monday, allowing a weak\ncold front to drop into the Lower Mississippi Valley. Very hot\ntemperatures along and ahead of this front will rise into the\nupper 90s to around 100 degrees. These hot temperatures combined\nwith pooling low level moisture will push heat index values\nacross east Arkansas, and areas east of the Mississippi river\ngenerally south of I-40 at or above 110 degrees, and 105 degrees\nacross the remainder of the area. Thus, additional Heat\nAdvisories and Extreme Heat Warnings may likely be needed into\nearly Monday evening with model solutions suggesting isolated to\nscattered convective development will occur mainly later Monday\nafternoon in a weakly sheared environment. Heat index values may\nremain elevated into Wednesday above 105 degrees across some\nlocations necessitating additional heat headlines.\n\nMid to long range ensemble and deterministic solutions indicate\nbetter chances for showers and thunderstorms and relief from the\noppressive heat will come later in the week into next weekend as\na mid-level trough settles in across the eastern U.S. This will\nbring a return of cooler temperatures towards normals for mid-\nAugust.",
